<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Euclid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 300 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Euclid was a Greek mathematician of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, active around 300 BCE, whose Elements became the foundational textbook of geometry. His work was translated into Arabic during the translation movement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, preserving and transmitting it to later Islamic and European scholars.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/euclid.jpg" alt="Euclid" width="311" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jusepe de Ribera (Spanish / Italian, 1591 - 1652) (1591 - 1652) – artist (Spanish / Italian) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Italian_-_Euclid_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Very little is known of Euclid's life, and most information comes from Proclus and Pappus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, writing many centuries later. According to Proclus he lived shortly after several of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>'s followers and before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archimedes">Archimedes</a>, during the rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I</a> (r. 305/304–282 BCE). His birth date and birthplace are unknown, and his death is speculated to have been about 270 BCE. He is often presumed to have been educated by Plato's disciples at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/platonic-academy">Platonic Academy</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, though Michalis Sialaros calls this mere conjecture. Pappus says Apollonius studied with Euclid's students in Alexandria, which has been taken to mean that Euclid taught there.</p><p>He is often called Euclid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> to distinguish him from Euclid of Megara, a pupil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a>, with whom he was conflated in medieval Byzantine sources. Medieval Arabic sources give a detailed biography, but it is unverifiable. The best-known anecdote, that he told Ptolemy there is no royal road to geometry, is questionable, since a similar story is told of Menaechmus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Euclid is best known for the Elements, a treatise in thirteen books. It is traditionally divided into plane geometry (books 1–6), basic number theory (books 7–10) and solid geometry (books 11–13). Much of its content comes from earlier mathematicians such as Eudoxus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippocrates-of-chios">Hippocrates of Chios</a>, Thales and Theaetetus. The classicist Markus Asper concludes that Euclid's achievement lay in assembling accepted knowledge into a cogent order and adding new proofs to fill gaps.</p><p>Book 1 begins with 20 definitions and ten assumptions, five postulates and five common notions, the fifth postulate being the parallel postulate. It includes the earliest surviving proof of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythagorean-theorem">Pythagorean theorem</a>. Book 7 contains the Euclidean algorithm for finding the common divisor of two numbers, and book 9 the proposition, now called Euclid's theorem, that there are infinitely many prime numbers. The Optics, the earliest surviving Greek treatise on perspective, the Data and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phaenomena">Phaenomena</a> also survive. The Conics, Pseudaria, Porisms and Surface Loci are lost.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Euclid is generally ranked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archimedes">Archimedes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollonius-of-perga">Apollonius of Perga</a> among the leading mathematicians of antiquity. The system of the Elements dominated geometry until the early 19th century, when non-Euclidean geometries were discovered. The Elements is often considered, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, the most frequently translated, published and studied book in the Western world, and it was the dominant mathematical textbook in the medieval Arab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> worlds. Its first English edition appeared in 1570, published by Henry Billingsley and John Dee.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Euclid” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The House of Wisdom (Bayt al-Hikma), traditionally associated with Harun al-Rashid and reaching its zenith under al-Ma'mun, sponsored the systematic translation of Greek, Persian, Indian and Syriac texts into Arabic; the project preserved Aristotle, <b>Euclid</b>, Galen and Claudius Ptolemy for the medieval world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It was home to the Library of Alexandria and the Mouseion, which attracted scholars such as <b>Euclid</b>, Eratosthenes and Herophilus, and to the Pharos lighthouse, one of the Seven Wonders of the ancient world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek literature and thought form one of the foundations of Western culture, and their influence reached the Islamic world, whose scholars translated Aristotle, Galen and <b>Euclid</b> into Arabic in the 8th to 10th centuries.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Humanists printed Archimedes, <b>Euclid</b> and Ptolemy in Greek, and the recovery of rival ancient schools, such as the atomism of Lucretius, showed that the ancients had disagreed among themselves; voyagers found lands, peoples, plants and animals unknown to the ancients; and printing put accurate drawings, maps and tables…</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The court also kept scholars and poets: Ptolemy II paid stipends to Callimachus and Theocritus, the mathematician <b>Euclid</b> worked under his patronage, and the chief librarian of the Library of Alexandria was also the crown prince’s tutor.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ptolemaic-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ptolemaic Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>An electric traffic signal with red and green lights was installed at <b>Euclid</b> Avenue and East 105th Street in Cleveland, Ohio.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-5">5 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
